## Novel Insights

The paper inadvertently provides a useful negative result: cluster-guided auxiliary classification—intuitively appealing as a way to align audio with semantic text topics—consistently hurts retrieval performance when added on top of a well-trained contrastive + distillation system. This suggests that coarse semantic topic signals may conflict with the fine-grained similarity structure learned by contrastive objectives, a finding the authors should have analyzed and reported rather than obscuring with overclaimed language.

## Suggestions

- Remove the fabricated claim "ablations indicate consistent improvements under high correspondence ambiguity" from the abstract and replace it with an honest summary of the actual findings.
- Revise the contributions list to distinguish between adopted techniques (distillation, LLM-mix) and the genuinely novel element (cluster guidance), and acknowledge that cluster guidance did not improve results.
- Add at least one external baseline (e.g., the DCASE 2024 Task 8 baseline or a CLAP model) to contextualize the reported numbers.
- Conduct and report an ablation varying cluster count and λ₂ to determine whether cluster guidance can work under different settings, or provide analysis of why it hurts.

