## Novel Insights

The paper reveals a fundamental symmetry-breaking opportunity in interventional causal discovery: post-treatment selection and genuine causal relations, while producing identical endpoint-level invariance patterns, differ in their internal path structure. Intervening on intermediate nodes along inducing paths exploits this structural asymmetry to disambiguate cases that are provably indistinguishable from endpoint CI patterns alone. This insight—that the "internal wiring" of an inducing path, not just its endpoints, carries identifiable information under interventions—is the conceptual core of the contribution and has no direct precedent in the interventional causal discovery literature.

