PA-TFNP sits between the low-scoring PACER/EllipWeather (flawed physics claims, limited contributions) and the medium-scoring DeepPrim/STORM (real contributions but significant methodological issues). The paper has genuine geometric and numerical contributions (spherical gradients, boundary conditions, TFN architecture) with consistent empirical improvements. However, the "physics-aware" framing is significantly overclaimed (wrong base equations for most variables, normalization stripping physical meaning), the 78.92% headline is cherry-picked and misleading, and the state-of-the-art claim is unsupported. These are not fatal flaws—the model works and the geometric contributions are real—but they substantially weaken the paper's central narrative. PA-TFNP is slightly below GSNO (4.0) due to the more severe overclaiming, and well above PACER (2.0) due to genuine contributions.

